1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,660
High on the north side of Mount Everest, climbers used to pass a strange landmark: a pair of bright green boots.

2
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:15,400
They belonged to a climber who died in nineteen ninety-six. For decades, he stayed exactly where he fell.

3
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:20,780
He is not alone. Many of the people who died on Everest are still up there.

4
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:24,730
So why are bodies left on the highest mountain on Earth?

5
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:28,850
Mount Everest stands on the border between Nepal and China.

6
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:38,450
In twenty twenty, the two countries announced its official height together: eight thousand, eight hundred and forty-eight point eight six metres.

7
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:47,200
In Nepal it's called Sagarmatha. In Tibetan, it's Chomolungma, often translated as Goddess Mother of the World.

8
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:52,410
Above about eight thousand metres begins what climbers call the death zone.

9
00:00:52,860 --> 00:01:02,630
The idea goes back to a Swiss doctor, Edouard Wyss-Dunant, who wrote in nineteen fifty-three about a lethal zone high on the great mountains.

10
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:08,990
At the summit, the air holds less than a third of the oxygen pressure you breathe at sea level.

11
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:14,470
Up there, the human body cannot adapt. The longer you stay, the weaker you get.

12
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:23,500
In a study published in two thousand nine, doctors took blood samples from climbers at about eight thousand four hundred metres.

13
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:31,740
Their blood oxygen was so low that, at sea level, doctors would only expect to see it in patients close to death.

14
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:40,780
In the thin air, fluid can build up in the lungs or the brain. Thinking slows, judgement fails, and fingers and toes freeze.

15
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:47,330
That's why most climbers carry bottled oxygen, and why every hour in the death zone counts.

16
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:54,570
The Himalayan Database counts three hundred and thirty-nine deaths on Everest up to twenty twenty-five.

17
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:59,520
In the same years, there were more than thirteen thousand seven hundred summits.

18
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:07,010
Two hundred and twenty-nine of those deaths were on the Nepal side, and one hundred and ten on the Tibet side.

19
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:12,000
That's roughly one death for every hundred times someone reaches the top.

20
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:19,810
Avalanches and falls cause the most deaths, followed by altitude sickness, exhaustion and the cold.

21
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:22,610
So why aren't they all brought down?

22
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:34,750
First, weight. A frozen body can weigh more than a hundred kilograms. Above eight thousand metres, even the strongest Sherpa climbers can carry only about twenty-five.

23
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:45,270
Second, ice. Bodies freeze into the mountain. In one recovery, it took eleven hours just to free one, using ice axes and boiling water.

24
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:54,190
Third, time. In twenty twenty-four, moving one body three and a half kilometres to the nearest camp took twenty-four hours.

25
00:02:54,640 --> 00:03:01,510
Fourth, weather. Good conditions high on Everest come only in short windows, about two weeks a year.

26
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:07,870
Fifth, cost. Oxygen alone for one recovery can cost around twenty thousand dollars.

27
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:17,730
And sixth, danger. In nineteen eighty-four, a Nepali police inspector and a Sherpa fell to their deaths while trying to bring down a body.

28
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:21,380
So some of the dead became part of the route itself.

29
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:29,170
Green Boots lay in a small rock alcove on the northeast ridge, at about eight thousand five hundred metres.

30
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:33,820
For years, climbers used him as a marker of how high they were.

31
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:38,680
In twenty fourteen, his body was reportedly moved out of view.

32
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:51,120
And this year, a DNA test reported by India's border police identified him as Lance Naik Dorje Morup, a member of their nineteen ninety-six expedition.

33
00:03:51,570 --> 00:03:54,280
India is now planning to bring him home.

34
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:57,680
The mountain also keeps its oldest mystery.

35
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:06,430
In June nineteen twenty-four, British climbers George Mallory and Andrew Irvine disappeared high on the northeast ridge.

36
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,250
Mallory's body was found in nineteen ninety-nine.

37
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:20,170
In twenty twenty-four, a National Geographic team found a boot on the glacier below. Inside was a sock with a label: A. C. Irvine.

38
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:27,300
Did they reach the top, twenty-nine years before Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary? No one knows.

39
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:31,090
Now, Nepal is starting to bring more of the dead home.

40
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:37,940
In twenty nineteen, a clean-up campaign brought down four bodies and eleven tonnes of rubbish.

41
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:46,690
In twenty twenty-four, soldiers and Sherpas recovered four bodies and a skeleton, along with about eleven tonnes of waste.

42
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:54,310
And as the snow and ice grow thinner, more bodies are appearing, in the Khumbu Icefall and at the South Col.

43
00:04:54,760 --> 00:05:03,890
Between twenty nineteen and twenty twenty-four, four campaigns brought down twelve bodies and around one hundred and eighty tonnes of rubbish.

44
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:07,260
Meanwhile, the mountain is busier than ever.

45
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:16,850
In spring twenty twenty-six, a record one thousand and eight summits were made from Nepal, according to Nepal's Department of Tourism.

46
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:28,320
Crowding is not new. In May twenty nineteen, a photo of a long queue of climbers near the summit went around the world. Eleven people died on Everest that season.

47
00:05:28,770 --> 00:05:36,950
On the busiest days, long lines of climbers wait in the death zone, using up time and oxygen they cannot get back.

48
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:44,780
Before every expedition, Sherpa climbers hold a puja at base camp, asking the mountain for safe passage.

49
00:05:45,230 --> 00:05:52,180
For them, Everest is not just a peak. It's a goddess, and those who never came down are part of its story.

50
00:05:52,630 --> 00:06:00,270
So the next time you see a summit photo, remember: the hardest part of Everest isn't getting up. It's getting back down.

51
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:04,670
Would you ever climb into the death zone? Tell us in the comments.

52
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:12,820
Next time: the tiny airport at the gateway to Everest, often called the most dangerous in the world. Lukla.

53
00:06:13,270 --> 00:06:15,460
This is Himalaya Unfolded.

